import{_ as t,o as a,c as o,a2 as s}from"./chunks/framework.2af478d7.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"0002. Typed terms search the native collections; the index serves filters and flags","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"adr/0002-search-on-native-collections-with-index-for-flags.md","filePath":"adr/0002-search-on-native-collections-with-index-for-flags.md"}'),i={name:"adr/0002-search-on-native-collections-with-index-for-flags.md"};function n(r,e,d,c,l,h){return a(),o("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[s('<h1 id="_0002-typed-terms-search-the-native-collections-the-index-serves-filters-and-flags" tabindex="-1">0002. Typed terms search the native collections; the index serves filters and flags <a class="header-anchor" href="#_0002-typed-terms-search-the-native-collections-the-index-serves-filters-and-flags" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;0002. Typed terms search the native collections; the index serves filters and flags&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Status: accepted</p><h2 id="context" tabindex="-1">Context <a class="header-anchor" href="#context" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Context&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The Drupal Search API index (<code>index/default</code>) matches whole words only (&quot;class&quot; does not find &quot;Classics&quot;), and it is the only place that holds boolean index fields and a total count in <code>meta.count</code>. The native JSON:API collections support <code>CONTAINS</code>, so they match partial words, but filtering them on a related entity&#39;s name joins the relationship and silently drops entities that have none. Neither source alone gives the search we want.</p><h2 id="decision" tabindex="-1">Decision <a class="header-anchor" href="#decision" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Decision&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>A typed term is matched on the native collections for every tab: <code>CONTAINS</code> per word on the type&#39;s <code>term_fields</code>. <code>filter[fulltext]</code> is not sent for typed terms.</li><li>A type that also matches related names (for example a talk and its speakers, topics and tracks) sends one native request per related path, merged and scored in PHP by the weights, so a related-name join cannot hide a title match.</li><li>A search with filters but no term goes to the index. Boolean index fields that have no native path exist only in the index, so with a term the index supplies the set of matching ids and the term matches are intersected with it.</li><li>Tab counts are fetched concurrently with the shown page (<code>DrupalClient::pool()</code>), and the search cache (60 s, fresh only) stores each request.</li><li>Suggestions use the native collections directly, uncached.</li></ul><h2 id="consequences" tabindex="-1">Consequences <a class="header-anchor" href="#consequences" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Consequences&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>Partial matching and relevance ranking work, at the cost of one request per related path and one per other tab.</li><li>Each native request is capped at 50 results (the JSON:API page size ceiling), so term matches beyond 50 are unreachable and term-search pagination is an in-memory slice of that list. Counts are the number of matches found, not the true total.</li><li>Entries that fail a condition are removed in PHP after fetching unless the rule is <code>filterable</code>, because a computed value cannot be filtered on in native collections.</li><li>The index lookup reads up to <code>search.index_lookup_pages</code> pages (10 by default, 500 ids). Beyond that, matching items are missed.</li><li>Relevance is approximated by the search fetcher&#39;s weights, which mirror the index&#39;s but are maintained by hand.</li><li>Moving to an index that matches partial words (e.g. n-grams) would let typed terms use <code>filter[fulltext]</code> again and remove the merge logic. It needs a backend change.</li></ul><h2 id="addendum-the-match-strategy-is-configurable" tabindex="-1">Addendum: the match strategy is configurable <a class="header-anchor" href="#addendum-the-match-strategy-is-configurable" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Addendum: the match strategy is configurable&quot;">​</a></h2><p>When the search generalises into a reusable package, the strategy becomes a per-project setting: <code>native_contains</code> (this ADR, the default) or <code>index_fulltext</code> for a backend whose index matches partial words. The Search API index is required for search under either strategy.</p><h2 id="update-2026-10-25" tabindex="-1">Update (2026-10-25) <a class="header-anchor" href="#update-2026-10-25" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Update (2026-10-25)&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The decision stands. The search code is now in the package and generic:</p><ul><li><code>SearchFetcher</code> replaces <code>SearchFetchService</code>, with <code>SearchTabs</code>, <code>SearchSorts</code>, <code>SearchFilterSet</code> and <code>SearchQueries</code> in place of the earlier tab, sort and filter classes.</li><li>The relevance weights are the <code>term_fields</code> and <code>related_term_fields</code> of the searchable content type, not constants in code.</li><li>Tabs, filters, sorts, page size and the 60-second search cache are descriptor settings under <code>search</code> and the content types. See the <a href="./../search-setup">Search setup</a> page.</li></ul><h2 id="update-2-0-0" tabindex="-1">Update (2.0.0) <a class="header-anchor" href="#update-2-0-0" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Update (2.0.0)&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The decision stands. Search now works across any number of searchable content types:</p><ul><li>Every searchable type, Primary or Facet, follows one matching path. The strategy is the global <code>search.match_strategy</code> unless the type sets its own <code>match_strategy</code>.</li><li>The index lookup is per type, capped by <code>search.index_lookup_pages</code>.</li><li>Tabs are the searchable types, in <code>search.tabs</code> order or descriptor order. A filter and a sort belong to the type that declares them and apply on its tab only; switching tabs drops what the new tab does not declare.</li><li>Suggestions query every searchable type, with quotas keyed by type.</li></ul><h2 id="update-3-0-0" tabindex="-1">Update (3.0.0) <a class="header-anchor" href="#update-3-0-0" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Update (3.0.0)&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The decision stands for typed terms, and the addendum&#39;s line that the Search API index is required is reversed: the index is no longer part of the package.</p><ul><li><code>SearchBackend</code> plans each tab (<code>plan()</code>, <code>capabilities()</code>) and <code>SearchFetcher</code> sends the plans as one concurrent batch. <code>NativeSearchBackend</code> is the default binding; the <code>proteo/drupal-search-api</code> package wraps it with <code>extend()</code> and adds the index paths. <code>NativeQueries</code> replaces <code>SearchQueries</code> for the native halves of <code>equals</code>, <code>year_range</code> and <code>boolean</code>.</li><li>A search with filters and no term is answered natively, with a real <code>page[offset]</code>. A native collection has no total, so counts carry an <code>exact</code> flag and read <code>N+</code> until <code>links.next</code> says the last page was seen. <code>Listing::total</code> is nullable.</li><li>A typed term keeps the behaviour of this record: one request per path, merged and scored in PHP, capped at <code>search.native_limit</code> per request. The cap is detected from <code>links.next</code>, not from the count equalling the limit.</li><li>A boolean without a native path is a validation error unless the bound backend declares <code>indexOnlyBooleans</code>. <code>index_fulltext</code>, <code>search.index_lookup_pages</code> and the other index keys are removed from core; they belong to the extension. See <a href="./../upgrading#_2-0-0-to-3-0-0">Upgrading</a>.</li></ul>',19)])])}const f=t(i,[["render",n]]);export{u as __pageData,f as default};
